# nanodregg — the charter (2026-10-16)

Ember: "rebuild whatever shape you think will be better. Go hardcore. ONLY GOOD CODE AND BEST
PRACTICES. All decisions get critically and metacritically examined."

nanodregg is the same end-computing system as minidregg (main 2c27b08b) rebuilt from its spec,
not ported from its code. The spec is: the journey ROWS, the deterministic SIMULATION, THE BAR
(two friends + hosted Hermes over ssh; 1000 records, write ≤ 5 s, cold reopen ≤ 60 s), and the
THEOREM STATEMENTS that mean something (not the vacuous ones; see the discovery dossiers). The
target shape is minidregg's `docs/HP-DESIGN.md` taken literally, and the reorientation documents
in `docs/record/`: `ARCHITECTURE-20261016.md`, `CYCLE-TIME-20261016.md`,
`RECOVERY-20261016.md`, and each `DELETE-<layer>-20261016.md`.

## Scope (Ember, 2026-10-21)

Ember: "we agree that nano has nothing to do with proof systems, host-hiding, or multi-node
agreement." nanodregg is an object of study more than the seed of a prototype: a side exploration,
for diversity and focus, while breadstuffs (the whole system built before it) waits to be resumed
or composted into the next robigalia. It is a single-node vat with one writer per Store, members
who consent by re-execution, and an operator who sees every byte. It demonstrates where minidregg
could be better where that is cheap. Two demonstrations are approved: documents and escrow. No
other minidregg aspect is pulled in to demonstrate.

Three things are ruled, not omitted: the first two are outside nanodregg, the third bounds it.

1. **Proof systems.** Nothing. Admission is re-execution; the proof slot stays empty; no circuit,
   STARK, folding scheme or accumulator is built here or enters the TCB. breadstuffs holds that
   lineage.
2. **Hiding from the Host.** Nothing. The operator is a trusted vat: the Host and every key holding
   a follow grant see every byte; membership is the confidentiality boundary; a member is kept out
   of a World by holding no follow grant (decisions/protocol.md PROTOCOL-3, ruling (a)).
3. **Writers.** One writer per World; followers re-derive every Turn (law 1, PROTOCOL-4). Many parties and
   many processes over one writer are in scope: residents, member sockets, followers, and the protocols
   between objects, with the member socket modelled. Many Worlds are in scope: a protocol between Worlds
   runs on receipts checked against held heads; it has no atomic cross-World commitment. Replication of a
   World's writer by agreement is a planned, priced phase that keeps one decided log per World; liveness is
   measured, never proved. Merging divergent histories of one World is refused: a fork is a new World.
   (Ember, real 2026-10-22, approving `redregg/work/PROGRAM-20261022.md` §2(d); decisions/lang.md LANG-75.)

A lane briefed to "add a proof", "hide it from the Host", "merge two histories of a World", "replicate
the writer" outside its planned phase, or "bring in another minidregg feature as a demo" is briefed
against this section: the brief is wrong, not the charter. The meta-critique below applies here too: a
scope chosen to avoid a hard part is the smell. This one is chosen because each excluded part is a
measured failure of the trees before it: breadstuffs' soundness apex vacuous at BabyBear, minidregg's
empty proof slot, an agreement mesh with one host.

## Laws (every commit obeys; a reviewer may revert on sight)

1. **One of everything.** One World, one Turn, one admission path (`World.admit` IS the receive
   path), one Store format, one codec family, one digest type (32 fixed bytes), one dispatch over
   one operation table, one sandbox shape, one build. A second shape of an existing thing is a
   defect, not a transition. Old SHAPES refuse to load; STATE may be carried only by re-genesis
   from a checked export (export = the fold, the World a Store opens to; import = genesis under a
   new epoch whose initial World is that World's cells re-sealed; check = canonical World bytes equal
   up to the epoch header; every signature over old bytes is NOT carried, so history is never
   carried, only state); re-genesis is free (OPS-1, accepted 10-17).
2. **Lean decides, Rust moves bytes.** Every accept/refuse/select/order/digest decision is a Lean
   definition with a named theorem. Rust (if any) is transport, custody, sockets, sandbox plumbing,
   and consumes Lean-generated tables (opcodes, bounds), never hand-copied constants. A PLATFORM
   FACT (an OS sandbox floor: which paths a process must see to start at all) is a table in the layer
   that touches the platform, not a decision; it carries a plant proving it does not over-allow (a
   file nobody named must be unreadable) and a recorded bisection for its minimality (CLIENT-9,
   accepted 10-16; CLIENT-2's correction: the plant proves no-over-allow, the bisection minimality).
3. **Bytes end to end.** `ByteArray` everywhere; no `List UInt8` in a deployed path; one-pass
   canonical decoders with a canonicity proof; content-addressed tags.
4. **TigerStyle.** A static bound on everything (`Bounds`); an assertion for every PREMISE the
   types do not carry (decoded bytes, disk reads, FFI results, anything that crosses a boundary),
   placed where the premise enters, each paired with the theorem that relies on it (KERNEL's K1
   restatement, accepted 10-16: inside a total Lean definition an "invariant assertion" only
   re-checks the compiler); no `partial def` in deployed code; no unbounded recursion; no
   allocation per byte; O(touched) work per request, never O(store). The resident's top-level
   fuel-and-restart loop is TOTAL: bounded by a LIFETIME constant (fuelBatches × restartsMax, larger
   than the reader's 2^48-frame lifetime) that no physical run can exhaust, with the theorem that
   exhausting it exits by name, never silently; no `partial` anywhere (HOST-19, blessed 10-17;
   supersedes the 10-16 "one unbounded loop" wording). A resource whose LIFETIME is the guarantee (a lock, a lease, a
   PID-file descriptor) lives in the value the running code holds, never in a separate local kept by
   a dummy later use, and a plant against the LIVE process refutes its early release: compiled Lean
   frees a value at its last use, which dropped the writer lock mid-serve while the lock was a local
   (HOST 7e23642; the plant was a second writer). The writer lock is the key file's read handle, a
   field of the `Writer` every effect of the loop holds (HOST-64, HOST-76); store-plants
   `seal-live-writer-locked` and `follow-live-locked` are its teeth (a second process against a live
   `host serve` / `host follow` is refused `locked`; RED with the lock removed).
5. **Performance is designed.** The resident Host is one deterministic loop per Store holding the
   World, the trie and the memo; admission is batched; the follower is the only replayer. "Checkpoint
   = World" means the World is what a checkpoint DENOTES. O(store) work is forbidden on the REQUEST
   path; off-loop sealing of a whole-World base that amortises to O(appended bytes) is allowed, with
   its cadence a named Bound, a `worldBytesMax` bound that refuses by name where the design must
   change, and a WASTE line that keeps the amortisation visible (HOST-14, measured 10-16: the encode
   is 25–35× cheaper than one fsync at THE BAR; it replaced K1's incremental deltas). HOST and CODEC
   own that representation. Measure
   at range ends with `perf stat --no-scale`; the waste ratio of every operation is pinned and may
   only fall.
6. **Named theorems, never `#guard`.** `theorem … := by decide/rfl`; the ONE tree-wide axiom census
   (`Census/Axioms.lean`, gate `gates/census.py`) replaces per-theorem `#assert_axioms` lines, because
   it checks every declaration rather than the ones someone remembered (GATES-4, accepted 10-16);
   only where the compiler must be trusted, `native_decide` with an allow row in
   `gates/axioms-allow.tsv`, which is its record. No `sorry` ever lands. Every
   theorem statement is read by a second agent before it lands (vacuity check: identity carriers,
   `P → P`, empty premises).
7. **Refusals by name.** Every refusal is a constructor with a name on the wire; a parse failure is
   never rendered as a verdict; "uncertain" is a real outcome.
8. **Small modules, split proofs.** A module exports definitions OR proofs about them, not both,
   so a proof edit rebuilds nothing downstream; the split is BY FILE (a `Proofs` path component),
   because Lean v4.30.0's `module` system still rebuilds importers on a proof-only edit (GATES-2,
   measured 10-16). "No import hubs" is measured as the SIZE metric `max dependents of one module`
   with a ceiling (GATES-6). Target: the Host closure under 150 modules; a one-module edit rebuilds
   its dependents in under 2 minutes on a laptop.
9. **Every commit builds.** `lake build` of the commit's import closure (and `cargo check` where
   Rust exists) passes BEFORE push. Main is the only branch. Small commits, often. A red on main is
   fixed forward within the hour by whoever sees it; `git revert` is always allowed.
10. **Decisions are examined, then re-examined.** Every design decision goes in `decisions/<layer>.md` (one file per layer; `DECISIONS.md` is frozen after 10-16: seven writers on one append-only file collided twice):
    the decision, the alternatives, why this one, the CRITIQUE (what would make it wrong), and the
    META-CRITIQUE (what bias produced it: familiarity with minidregg's shape, an estimate mistaken
    for a constraint, a phase ordering chosen to avoid a hard part). A decision without both
    critiques is not made.
11. **No twins of minidregg.** Reading minidregg is allowed to learn the spec and the mistakes;
    copying a file is not. If a minidregg definition is right, restate it from its theorem, smaller.
    Carve-out (PROTOCOL, 10-16): the conformance ROWS restate the minidregg journeys step for step,
    because the journeys ARE the spec; that is restating the spec, not copying code.
12. **Size is a ratchet.** `SIZE.md` holds the counts (modules, lines per layer, opcodes,
    constructors, Rust lines, partial defs, theorems); a commit that raises a count explains why in
    the message or is reverted.

## Layers and owners (the six rebuild agents)

Dependency order is top to bottom; a lower layer builds against the REAL signatures the layer
above has committed, never against prose.

| layer | owner | owns | first deliverable |
|---|---|---|---|
| KERNEL | DISCOVER-KERNEL → BUILD-KERNEL | `Kernel/`: World, Turn, Leg, History, `World.admit`, `fold`, Bounds, the Store model (log of Turns, checkpoint = World, roots), the simulation generator, AND `Kernel/Receive/`: the RECEIVERS (signed request → Turn: authority, law, funding; one derivation per operation kind, each a def with its theorem; derive-then-admit, the World's guard check is the one staleness refusal) — ROOT ruling 10-16 on HOST-1; HOST drives and decides nothing |
| LANG | BUILD-LANG | `Lang/`: Objective Bend — the Core4 term language, typing/checking, the demand machine, compilation of a checked term to an installed law / invocable package, and the hosted-resident surface (what a Hermes resident can author and install); minidregg's `Theory/ObjectiveBend*` (46 files) and `Compiler/ObjectiveBend*` (28) are the SPEC to restate smaller, never copy; consumes KERNEL's `install`/`invoke` receiver signatures | `Lang/Term.lean` + `Lang/Check.lean` with the preservation/determinism theorems, and one law compiled end to end through a KERNEL receiver | `Kernel/World.lean` + `Kernel/Turn.lean` + `Kernel/Bounds.lean` with the admit/fold theorems (determinism, follower soundness, charge exactness, guard-from-intent staleness refusal) and `Kernel/Sim.lean` (seeded generator + Host/follower side-by-side check) |
| CODEC | DISCOVER-CODEC → BUILD-CODEC | `Codec/`: the one `StreamCodec` family over ByteArray, `Digest256`, content-addressed tags, canonicity proofs, the generated operation table and bounds for Rust | `Codec/Stream.lean` with canonicity theorem, `Codec/Tag.lean`, and the generator for `protocol/*.json` |
| HOST | DISCOVER-HOST → BUILD-HOST | `Host/`: the resident loop (one per Store), batched admission, the single dispatcher over the op table, the durable Store (SQLite or a flat log: decide and critique), checkpoints, crash protocol, the consent provider as the SAME code path in a second process | `Host/Loop.lean` admitting Turns through `World.admit`, persisting through one append, with the crash-restart plant |
| PROTOCOL | DISCOVER-PROTOCOL → BUILD-PROTOCOL | `protocol/`: the operation table (target ≤ 40 ops), consent frames (merged), client commands, the SPK broker verbs; every op's receiver named; the conformance rows (the journeys rewritten as executable rows against nanodregg) | `protocol/operations.lean` (the table as data with a theorem that every op has exactly one receiver) + `rows/` (journey J0–J8, JPAY, jwell, m6grain, hermes as nanodregg rows) |
| CLIENT | DISCOVER-RUST → BUILD-CLIENT | `client/` (Rust, one crate, one process over two sockets: custody + transport + workspace; decides nothing) and `sandbox/` (bwrap launcher, SPK broker, grain runtime as thin plumbing over Lean-decided policy) | `client/` that performs challenge → prepare → assemble → submit → lookup against BUILD-HOST's loop, with the generated op table as its only protocol knowledge |
| CLOSURE | DISCOVER-CLOSURE → BUILD-GATES | `gates/` + the build: lakefile with small modules and the def/proof split enforced, the size ratchet, the waste ratchet, the import-closure gate, `#assert_axioms` census, the standing service script (tip → build → sim → rows), the CI that runs on a laptop | `lakefile`, `gates/size`, `gates/closure` (refuses a def-module importing a proof-module), `SIZE.md` seeded, `scripts/standing.sh` |
| HOSTING | BUILD-HOSTING | `Hosting/` (lifecycle generator + theorems, Plan, controller + broker decisions with the lease, resident profile), the `launcher/` Rust crate, `world/grain/` + `world/hosting/` package texts, `decisions/hosting.md`; must never hold a member seed in a process that parses model/provider bytes, keep grain phase/pid/volumes as truth outside the World, or add a Body constructor/receiver without KERNEL (full row: `docs/record/ASSEMBLY-hosting.md` §4) | the grain lifecycle green through `Receive.submit`; the broker's first refusal by name with no key reaching the resident |
| LEDGER | BUILD-LEDGER (a KERNEL deliverable, prefix `kernel:`) | `Kernel/Value.lean`, `Kernel/Proofs/Value.lean`, `Kernel/Receive/Ledger.lean`, `Kernel/Proofs/Ledger.lean`, the ledger arms of `Kernel/Sim/Gen.lean`; edits Cell/World/Call/Derive only by agreement with KERNEL (full row: `ASSEMBLY-ledger.md`) | value conservation as an equality with its plant; floors + wells (pug's rule); escrow never both released and refunded |
| OPS | BUILD-OPS | `Ops/` (unit renderer emitted with `--check`, Node values, the BAR driver), `ops/` (units, RUNBOOK, live/), `decisions/ops.md`; must never decide what HOST/KERNEL/CLIENT decide, touch a service manager outside its scratch, hold key material, or delete a Store (full row: `ASSEMBLY-ops.md`) | units emitted + live harness green against the real `host`; world-carry round trip with its check |
| PERF | BUILD-PERF (a lane, not a decision layer) | `Bench/` (C9 tooling), `scripts/bar.sh`, `gates/bar.tsv`, `decisions/perf.md`; must never decide anything, copy a deployed definition, quote a toy/warm/extrapolated number as THE BAR, or add a wall-clock row to waste.tsv (full row: `ASSEMBLY-perf.md`) | THE BAR over sockets on a quiet machine, with load recorded |
| TRANSPORT | BUILD-TRANSPORT (temporary; dissolves when D1–D7 land) | nothing permanently: drives store identity, the mux close, thin-consent plumbing into the owners' paths under their prefixes (full row: `ASSEMBLY-transport-consent.md`) | D1+D2: a request and an envelope bound to their Store (flag day), with the foreign-segment plant |
| SPEC | SPEC (a ROOT lane) | `Spec/` (the top-level claims over the deployed entry points: `System`, the trivial systems, each claim with its top theorem, floor and deployed run), `decisions/spec.md`; must never restate a deployed definition (a field of `deployed` names it) or land a claim without a floor | every TOPLEVEL-SPEC claim proved of `deployed` and refused by a trivial system |

The 10-17 rows (HOSTING, LEDGER, OPS, PERF, TRANSPORT) came from the scoutotype swarm (27 Sonnet scouts → 6 Opus assemblies, recorded as `docs/record/ASSEMBLY-*.md`); the primary checkout `~/dev/nanodregg` is the READ-ONLY reference at main, moved by `scripts/commit` after each landing.

Until KERNEL commits its first signatures, the other five write their layer's `DESIGN.md`
(decisions with critiques) and their interface PROPOSAL as Lean/Rust signatures in a
`proposals/<layer>.md`, and build whatever does not depend on the kernel (CODEC's StreamCodec,
CLOSURE's lakefile and gates, CLIENT's socket/custody skeleton, PROTOCOL's rows as specs). KERNEL
reads the proposals before committing signatures and answers each in `DECISIONS.md`.

## Working rules

- Repo: `~/dev/nanodregg` (git, branch `main`, no other branches); remote `origin` = github.com/emberian/nanodregg (public, created by Ember's order 10-17). Until scripts/commit pushes after landing (a GATES successor item), ROOT pushes main to origin at each report. Commit messages: what, why, what
  it refuses, with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>` (unsigned is fine).
- Build on the Mac: `lake build` / `cargo check` of your import closure before every push. Keep
  `.lake` small; the Mac data volume is nearly full: no large artifacts, no fixtures over 1 MB, no
  copies of minidregg. hbox is Ember's: do not use it. No box builds until ROOT says.
- Seven agents, ONE REPO, SEPARATE WORKTREES (ruled 10-16 after six collisions in one shared
  checkout): every layer edits in its own `git worktree` (`scripts/worktree <layer>` →
  `~/dev/nanodregg-wt/<layer>`, same `.git`, same `refs/heads/main`); the primary checkout
  `~/dev/nanodregg` is nobody's editing surface. Commit ONLY with `scripts/commit -m MSG PATH...`
  from your worktree: it builds the candidate from CURRENT main plus exactly your paths, runs the
  gates, and lands on main by compare-and-swap (retrying when main moved). A plain `git commit` is
  refused by the hook. Commit small, commit only your layer's files, never `git add -A`, never
  `git stash`, never rewrite history. THE SHARED INDEX IS NOT YOURS: never stage into the default index (another agent's
  stale index reverted a charter commit on 10-16, 65e3c1c → fixed forward feeaed3). Either commit
  with explicit paths (`git commit --only`) or use a private index (`GIT_INDEX_FILE=$(mktemp)`,
  `git read-tree HEAD`, add your paths, `write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref`). Before every
  push, `git show --stat HEAD` must list ONLY your files; a commit that touches another layer's file
  or reverts someone's hunk is a defect: fix forward, tell the owner.
- The primary checkout `~/dev/nanodregg` is the READ-ONLY reference (scouts, evaluators, `lake env lean` against its .lake); `scripts/commit` refuses to commit from it and moves it to each new main when clean. Lane-private file names in the shared scratchpad (a message file overwritten by another lane was judged under the wrong subject).
- Lakefile rule (GATES): add your `lean_lib` entry (`globs = ["X.+"]`) in the SAME commit as your
  first `.lean` file; a glob over a directory missing from the commit breaks everyone's build. A conflict in a shared file (lakefile, DECISIONS.md, SIZE.md) is resolved by reading
  both sides. Talk to the owner of a layer you need something from (SendMessage); do not build
  your own copy of their thing.
- Tests are theorems, plants and rows. A plant is a mutation that goes red by name: a vacuity check
  on a theorem or a gate, run when it is cheap. It is not required of every theorem, it is not a
  landing gate, and a plant line in a commit is a courtesy, not a rule (Ember, 2026-10-24). A
  landing goes when its builder's build and tests are green; the whole system is re-judged by a
  convergence per batch. No unfiltered test suites.
- Process rules from minidregg still apply: PID file or nothing for kills; every loop sleeps and
  has a deadline; disk pressure is Ember's (stop and report, delete nothing).
- Report to ROOT ("main") on events only: first signatures committed, a decision that another
  layer must answer, a law you think is wrong (argue it in DECISIONS.md with both critiques; ROOT
  rules), your first green row, your handoff (write `<LAYER>-LOG.md` in `~/dev/redregg/work/` as
  your continuation before 350K tokens).

## The meta-critique that applies to this whole charter

It was written in one pass by ROOT, from the minidregg map, under the bias that minidregg's layer
names are the right names. The first job of each owner is to attack the row of this table they
own: if the layer should not exist, or should be two, or should be inside another, say so in
`DECISIONS.md` with the critique, and ROOT re-cuts the table. The second job is to attack the laws:
each is a response to a measured minidregg failure (cite the dossier), and a law that answers a
failure nanodregg cannot have is dead weight.
  - the decider pipes.
- The decider bound is LEAN's: `Hosting.Broker.Bounds.answerMsMax` (120 000 ms, a backstop above THE BAR's 60 s cold reopen), emitted as `ANSWER_MS_MAX`.
- `residents::Decider` is the one shape both consumers hold (CLIENT-29's "one consumer shape"). Every question is its frames written and one verdict read under one deadline. A decider that misses it, or sends bytes that do not decode, is LOST for good, because a late or half-read verdict would put the next answer out of step. Its consumer then ends by the decider's own end or `deciderLost`, as before. `KeybrokerExit.deciderLost`'s doc and its unit action now name the silent case.
- `fsx::create_once` writes `.NAME.tmp`, syncs it, and gives it its name by a HARD LINK, which refuses an existing name as `O_EXCL` did, then syncs the directory. `replace` shares the staging. `.NAME.tmp` is fsx's crash residue: the next write removes it, and the attempt scan passes over it.

**Plants** (`fixrust-scratch/plants3.log`, `plants3c.log`; each RED under its mutation).
- `client/tests/sockets.rs a_trickling_peer_is_a_timeout_within_one_reply_bound`: a header and then one byte per 100 ms is `timeout` within the 1 s bound. P3a: per-read timeouts → HANG past 5 s.
- `launcher/tests/broker.rs a_silent_or_stalled_decider_is_lost_within_the_answer_bound`: a silent decider and a half-frame decider are lost within 300 ms, and the next question is not asked. CONTROL: an answering one is read. P3c: no deadline on the read → HANG.
- `client/tests/attempt.rs a_killed_freeze_leaves_its_call_absent_or_whole`: a child freezing 4 MiB calls is SIGKILLed twenty times; every `call` present is whole. P3b: the in-place write → short calls (red three runs of three).
- `client/tests/custody.rs a_crash_inside_arm_leaves_the_workspace_unarmed`: the residue reads as not armed and the next `arm` succeeds. A short `rotation` made outside fsx still refuses by name.

**Critique.**
- The mux's reply-write bound has no plant of its own (it is the same `Deadline` the trickle plant reds). The keybroker upstream bound has none either: a trickling TLS upstream needs a test server, and the call goes through the same type.
- `PIPE_BUF` chunking costs one poll per 4 096 bytes on Linux (512 on macOS): 64 polls for a full 262 144-byte frame.
- The residents' own broker sockets (`RESIDENT_IDLE`, per read) are not changed. A resident is the client of its own grant, and the trust class is the member's, not a stranger's. If that changes, the same type applies.
- The bound for one decider answer is a constant, not a theorem about the decider. The decider's loop is total (fuel), but no Lean statement says an answer takes at most 120 s: it is the consumer's patience, named.

**Meta-critique.** The fix is ONE type, not three local timeouts, because the defect was one class (a per-syscall bound standing in for a per-reply one) found at four sites, and the mux already had the right shape privately. A future peer gets the bound by using the type, not by remembering it.

**Flag day.** A new emitted constant (`ANSWER_MS_MAX`, `launcher/src/broker_generated.rs`) and one changed unit-action text (`deploy/units/EXITS.md`). No wire or Store format changes: `create_once`'s files are byte-identical, only the way they come to exist changed.

**Status.** landed (this commit).

## CLIENT-40 · law 2 for the client's own bytes: every hand-read layout's widths are Lean's, the frame / child wire / rotation record are checked row by row against Lean's codec, and the hand-copied names and numbers are emitted (2026-10-24, FIX-RUST-2; readers/COLLATION-20261024 A17, HOLISTIC-CODEC §4.1–4.2, READ-RUST F16)

**The defects (READ at 7655145).**
- `client/src/frame.rs` (`HEADER = 4`), `mux.rs` (`CONN = 4`), `stdio.rs`, `join.rs` and `attach` (`saturating_add(4)`, `get(..4)`, `body[5..]`) each spelled the frame and child-wire layouts by hand. The mux built its reply and close frames itself, with `u32::try_from(len).unwrap_or(u32::MAX)`: a length that does not fit would have been written as a LIE (`u32::MAX`) rather than refused.
- `custody.rs` wrote and read the armed rotation as `u64le ‖ 32 ‖ 32`, a 72-byte record known only to Rust.
- Hand copies of Lean facts: the receipt line prefixes (`attempt.rs`, follow prints them in `Command.Machine.receiptText`), the socket names (`nd.rs`, `nd-members.rs`, `keybroker.rs`; `Ops.Units` again as strings), nd-members' restart policy (`2 s / 5 / 120 s`, citing the deleted `nd-member@`), the 200 ms decider tick (twice), `grain_name_ok`'s character class (Lean's `labelByte`), and "a code ≥ 128 is a signal" restated unconditionally in `residents.rs` and `grain-launch.rs`. The platform fact (`nanodregg_sandbox::killed_by_signal`) says that only on Linux.
- `client/tests/foreign.rs` X1b asserted only "failed"; the comment after X2h claimed a check (at the advanced head the receipt checks, refused as a second acceptance) that the test never made.

**Decision.**
- Widths from Lean, tied at compile time: `Protocol.Bounds.frameLenBytes` / `connBytes` (each `Stream.u32.size 0`), `childBodyMax` (`Host.Wire.bodyMax` is it), `Codec.Ed25519.publicKeyBytes` (`verify_pk_size`), emitted `FRAME_LEN_BYTES`, `CONN_BYTES`, `CHILD_BODY_MAX`, `PUBLIC_KEY_BYTES`. `frame.rs` asserts in `const` that both are a `u32`'s width; `custody.rs` that the key width is `ed25519_dalek`'s. A Lean width change fails the Rust BUILD, not a test.
- One writer and one reader of each layout: `frame::encode_body` is the only place a length prefix is written (`write_body` uses it). `frame::child_parts` / `child_frame` / `write_child` / `child_max` are the child wire for the mux, `nd --stdio`, init's follower and `nd-attach`. The mux's reply and close frames go through `encode_body`. A reply past the client bound ends the mux `ChildMalformed` by name; a close that does not frame is said on stderr and the connection is shut unnamed. A short child body is `FrameError::BadLength` (Host.Wire's `badLength`); `Truncated` carries Lean's offset.
- The rotation record is Lean's: `Protocol.rotationLayout` (`u64 attempt, bytes incoming, bytes pending`) with the codec `rotationFrame` and `rotationBytes` (80, `rotationFrame_size`). It is emitted as `layout::ArmedRotation` and `ROTATION_BYTES`; custody arms and reads through it.
- Bilateral vectors (HOLISTIC-CODEC §4.1's emitted-Rust leg, for these three layouts): sites `wire/frame` (`Protocol.socketFrame`, read by `Protocol.bodyParts?`), `wire/child` (`Host.childFrame` + `Host.decodeBody`, framed by `Host.encodeFrame`/`encodeReply`) and `client/rotation` in `Emit.Vectors`. Each has drawn rows and refusal rows (empty, past bound, truncated header, truncated body, trailing, a short child body, the 72-byte CLIENT-39-era record). `client/tests/vectors.rs` decodes every row with the deployed Rust reader, renders Lean's text (refusals at Lean's offsets), and re-encodes each accepted row with the deployed writer to the same bytes. Runs row `cargo:client:vectors` (`nobin ok`).
- Names and numbers emitted: `FOLLOW_SOCK`, `HOST_SOCK`, `MEMBER_SOCK`, `BROKER_SOCK` (Hosting.Plan; `hostSock` is new, and `Ops.Units` uses `hostSockName`/`followSockName`); `RECEIPT_WORD`, `HEAD_WORD`, `SETTLE_WORD` (`Protocol.Attempt`, which `receiptText` prints by); `DECIDER_TICK_MS` (`Hosting.Broker.Bounds.tickMs`); `RESTART_SECONDS`, `START_BURST`, `START_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (the restart policy, moved from `Ops.Units` to `Hosting` because nothing may import Ops (closure C10); the units render the same one); `LABEL_BYTES` (`Hosting.labelByte`): `grain_name_ok` = `!empty && wire::path_clean && ⊆ LABEL_BYTES`, Lean's `labelOk`.
- Signal deaths: `decider_end` and grain-launch's `ended` take the `ExitStatus` and ask `killed_by_signal`. grain-launch passes a controller's code through only when `ControllerExit` names it.
- foreign.rs: X1b asserts `keyTaken`. The claimed check is made: at the advanced head, a later receipt of the same call is refused `keyTaken` and not `headMismatch` (MEASURED green on hbox).

**Plant** (cheap, run once): `child_parts` reading the conn id big-endian → `every_child_wire_vector_reads_and_writes_as_lean_says` RED (`wire/child 0: re-encoded`).

**Critique.**
- The frame and mux are still hand-written Rust: what is Lean's is their widths, bounds and every row's verdict, not their code. HOLISTIC-CODEC §4.2 asks for emitted or deleted. The deadline and stream handling are not layout and stay hand-written. Emitting the byte part would mean a second string template (the thing §4.2 says to stop growing) until an emitter with a semantics exists.
- `Emit/Rust.lean`'s `frameIo` template (launcher/broker generated files) is a third u32 framing (`as u32`, a literal 4). It is emitted, so not hand Rust, but the vectors do not cover it. It is the obvious next site: `wire/frame` at `FRAME_MAX`.
- The three-way check is two-way here (compiled Lean writes the table; the Rust reads it). The kernel leg is the codecs' lawfulness theorems, not a decision over these rows.
- Tests that state a deployed layout (`launcher/tests/members.rs`'s socket listing, `isolation.rs`) keep their literals on purpose: a test that reads the constant cannot see a wrong constant. `resident_bell.rs`'s `PATIENCE` (Lang.Bell.patience) is a hand copy left as is. There is no batch-write verb, so its 2048 sequential writes stay (LANG's call, READ-RUST §4).
- `SameRecord`'s `keys` file (2–3 seeds, no lengths) is still a Rust-only layout. It is secret material and a fixed-width array, which `Layout` does not express.

**Meta-critique.** The test of each move was whether a Lean change would be FELT in Rust without anyone remembering. Widths fail the build; layouts fail the vectors; names and numbers change by re-emission. The weakest part is the first critique point. The vectors make a hand reader's disagreement visible, but they do not make it impossible.

**Flag day.** The custody rotation record is 80 bytes (`rotationLayout`). A 72-byte `ws/rotation` refuses `Length` by name: settle or remove it before upgrading a workspace with a rotation armed. Re-emitted: `client/src/generated.rs`, `launcher/src/generated.rs`, `launcher/src/broker_generated.rs`, `gates/codec-vectors.tsv` (three new sites, no existing row moved). Units are byte-identical.

**Status.** landed (this commit).

## CLIENT-41 · the testground: a member's client in a browser tab, its writer and follow compiled to WebAssembly (2026-10-26, TESTGROUND; redregg/work/promo/DESIGN-TESTGROUND.md)

**Decision.** `testground/` is a static page that plays `nd`'s part (framing, custody, the attempt loop) in JavaScript, and `Web/Sim/Testground.lean` is the kernel it talks to, compiled through Lean's C and emscripten into one module (`testground/build/build.sh`; MEASURED 10-26 at 202f8f4: 3.46 MB, 0.66 MB gzipped, a write admitted in 10–14 ms in Chromium). The module exports six byte functions: `tg_genesis` (`Receive.genesis` at the deployed `Ctx`, `Authority.none`, a Store as `Host.Sim.memGenesis` leaves it), `tg_host` (one frame through `Host.serve`, the deployed loop, over the in-memory Store, with the rows' honest `Protocol.Rows.Writer` machine/checkpoint/pager), `tg_follow` (`Host.Follow.answer` per founder), `tg_pub`/`tg_sign` (CODEC's Ed25519 for the page's custody), `tg_attempt` (`Protocol.Attempt.next`) and `tg_table` (`Protocol.Table` as JSON, read off its `all` lists). Their byte ABI is `Web/c/glue.c`, an `extern_lib` like `Host/c` (lake builds it with `-Werror`; no native executable references it). `testground/nd.js` holds no op code, status, step tag, layout, bound or refusal name: it reads them from `tg_table` of the kernel it loaded, so the page cannot hold a table older than its kernel (the genesis-authority flag day 8f1e332 changed no line of it). The module is tooling (`Sim` component): it is the rows' system in a tab and nothing deployed imports it.

**Alternatives.** (a) A TS file emitted by `Emit/` beside `generated.rs`: a second projection that can be stale against the kernel it is served with; the runtime table cannot. (b) A thin page over a WebSocket with the follow on a server: the visitor watches someone else re-execute. (c) The kernel threaded (`-pthread`): needs COOP/COEP headers GitHub Pages cannot send. (d) A new TESTGROUND layer: needs a subject prefix in `gates/owners.py` (a judge change); the page IS the member's client, so CLIENT owns it.

**Critique.** In the tab every party is the same honest program, so the property nano is for (members who consent by re-execution against a writer they do not trust) is invisible until the writer lies: the rows' plants (`Rows.Writer.Params`) as a switch are the next card. The page's refusal glossary is a hand table of names (it can go silent on a rename, never mislabel: the name shown is always follow's); its home is the constructors' docstrings. The toolchain's C is re-emitted from its sources (`-Dbackward.do.legacy=false`); one module (`Init.Data.String.PosRaw`) is taken from stage0 because its official IR names an auxiliary a re-emit lacks; `build.sh` links with undefined symbols an error, and a recorded trace of 368 kernel calls replays byte for byte on a native build. `kernel.wasm` is not committed: it re-emits on every change to its 105-module closure.

**Meta-critique.** The pull was to build a pretty explorer over a JSON dump of the World; that would have been a second renderer and a second reader of the bytes. The measurement came first because the fork (in-tab kernel or hosted World) decided whether a page could hold a follow at all; it can, and that made the hosted World one swapped function rather than a second client.

**Status.** landed (this commit). Ember publishes `testground/` after `build.sh`.

## CLIENT-42 · the lying writer: the testground's writer tells the conformance rows' own lies, and the visitor's follow refuses each by name (2026-10-26, TESTGROUND-2; cv 01a17a48-b31e; DESIGN-TESTGROUND §7)

**Decision.** The World window has a Writer menu. Each entry is a row's own plant, switched on in the kernel by `tg_lie`: a writer plant runs `Host.serve` under the `Rows.Writer.Params` that `Protocol.Rows.Loopback.sys` gives that mutation (`forgesRefusal`), and a relay fault puts the rows' relay between the members and the writer (`flipEvent`, `dropLastEvent`, `lieLookup`, `lieCancel`, `forgeCancel`, and the loss `neverForward` the cancel lies need first). So that the tab runs the rows' code and not a copy of it, the relay's lies are named functions of `Loopback` that its own `steps`/`drive` now call (`cancelLie`, `forwards`, `losesFirstReply`, `lookupLie`, beside the existing `pageFault`), and the hostile client's call is `Loopback.forgedCall`, which `forged` calls; `tg_forge` is that function over the page's custody seeds (a member lie: the forked self-head post, PROTOCOL-75). `tg_lies` is the menu: each lie with the row that plants it, the line, and the outcome that row expects there, read off `Protocol.rows` (a writer plant: the row whose `Plant.caught` is that mutation; a relay fault: the first line injecting it; a member lie: the row's first non-`ok` line after its first `forge`), so the page shows the row's expectation beside the visitor's own follow's verdict and writes neither. `tg_cold` starts a follow again from genesis (the rows' `cold`), `tg_text` serves a row's own package text (`Protocol.slot`), `tg_lied` says the relay rewrote the last frame (the Wire window marks it `LIED`, a forged call `FORGED`). Each lie has a scene (`demos.js`: the row's lines with alice as its sponsor, bob as the checker); the tour's sixth step is "now make the writer lie". `testground/lies-run.mjs` runs every scene under node: 7/7 end as their rows expect (eventMismatch, chainMismatch ×2, ok, hostContradicted cancelled open, cancelForged, forkedPost), and with the lies switched off (`--honest`) the five switchable ones end `ok`.

**Alternatives.** (a) The lies written in JavaScript over the frames: a second implementation of each plant, which can drift from the row and is exactly what a reader cannot check. (b) `Fault`/`Mutation` and the lies moved into a small module so the kernel need not import `Protocol.Rows`: the expectations would then be a copy; importing the rows costs 0.14 MB gzipped (MEASURED: 3.46 → 4.19 MB raw, 0.66 → 0.80 MB gzip) and is the price of reading them. (c) Every row runnable in the tab: rows bind four actors and joins; two founders and seven scenes are what a visitor needs.

**Critique.** The scenes' argv are hand-transposed from the rows (sponsor → alice, operator/auditor → bob), so a row that changes its lines leaves its scene behind; the node harness is the check that the scene still ends as the row expects, and nothing runs it in a gate yet. `lieTip`, `lieBase`, `forgeBase` and `admitsRecorded` are not in the menu: each needs a paging, a thin adopt or a recorded resubmit the tab cannot yet reach cheaply. A writer plant poisons the writer's log for good (switching back to honest leaves the forged record), which the page says.

**Meta-critique.** The pull was to make each lie a pretty animation; the lesson is the refusal NAME and the row's line beside it, so the dialog shows both and the frame that lied. Reading the expectation off the rows turned up that the rows count lines from 0 (the first draft printed every line one too far).

**Status.** landed (this commit). Re-emits: `testground/kernel.{mjs,wasm}` (`build.sh c` + `build.sh wasm`); nothing else.

## CLIENT-43 · the hosted World: the same page with the writer behind a WebSocket, the visitor a member joined over an invitation (2026-10-26, TESTGROUND-2; cv 01a17a48-b346; DESIGN-TESTGROUND §3.2)

**Decision.** The kernel's `Ground` holds its writer as an `Option`: a hosted World has none, and the page hands `client(K, { host })` a transport (`testground/transport.js`: host.sock's own frames, `u32le len ‖ op ‖ payload` out and `u32le len ‖ status ‖ payload` back, one request in flight, a dropped connection answered `closed unreachable` as the mux answers it) instead of `K.host`. The member's follow stays in the tab. It joins by `tg_join INVITATION KEY` (`Invite.accept` under the sponsor key the page trusts: the follow's first World is what the signed inputs make, never the writer's World handed over), runs its own `init SPONSOR`, and is enrolled by the guest-book (`testground/guestbook.mjs`: founder 0 of the hosted World, the page's own nd.js and kernel under node, its seeds in a 0600 file on the writer's box, answering one `join HEX` with its own `enroll` and `grant world … observe`); `tg_self` then sets the member's ids. `tg_found INPUT I` is a founder's follow from `host genesis`'s `input` line. `deploy/testground/ws-relay.py` (Python's standard library) moves bytes between a WebSocket and host.sock and parses no frame and no op (the writer refuses what it does not serve; the mux bounds the connection); it also serves the guest-book's `world.json` and forwards `POST /enroll`. `deploy/testground/run.sh` starts the three with PID files; the relay listens on loopback only. MEASURED 10-26 on hbox (`wt/testground-b`, `host serve` under `nd-supervise`, through an ssh tunnel to the laptop): two node members, two kernels, join + enrol 223–240 ms each; create/write/grant/write-through-the-grant 58–99 ms each; the document demo with alice and bob in two kernels against the one real writer, every step as scripted, the revoked candidate refused `program` by alice's own follow; in Chromium two tabs joined in 1.9 s and tab two's write through tab one's grant read back in tab one.

**Alternatives.** (a) A WebSocket face in Rust inside `client/` (the mux with a socket on one side): a hand-written RFC 6455 with SHA-1, or a new crate in the TCB, for a process nobody trusts; the relay decides nothing, so it is deployment glue under OPS's tree. (b) The guest-book's key in the page (anyone enrols anyone): the same bound, but the key could also rotate itself away; the guest-book keeps it on the box. (c) A viewer page with the follow on the server: the visitor watches someone else's check.

**Critique.** The guest-book enrols without asking anything: its bound is the kernel's (the founder grant's enrolments, the meter), which is the point and also the whole of the abuse control. The trust anchor is the page's publisher (kernel and sponsor key) until the kernel hash is reproducible and TRUST-SEED's certified heads give the visitor a writer certificate to check. The fork across two real tabs needs two writers of one genesis (a second `host genesis` with the same inputs): not built (cv). A hosted World has no lies yet: the relay lies of CLIENT-42 run in the tab's kernel writer only. The units are written, not yet run under systemd.

**Meta-critique.** The temptation was a relay that "helps" (parses ops, filters by the table, retries); every such help is a decision a member cannot check, so it moves bytes and names nothing.

**Status.** landed (this commit). Re-emits: `testground/kernel.{mjs,wasm}`.

