## CLIENT-59 · the page awaits its kernel (2026-10-27, PERF-TESTGROUND)

**Decision.** A dedicated module worker owns a World'\\''s kernel. The page sends byte packets containing a request id, the export name and its arguments. The worker serializes calls and returns the export'\\''s result as bytes. No protocol decision moves into the transport. The member client and teaching windows await the same API; node harnesses continue to use the direct kernel. Each writer reply carries the kernel'\\''s lie marker from that call, so another queued call cannot change its attribution. Worker failure rejects pending requests as a transport error. Closing a World releases its worker. The page supplies the WASM bytes it hashed, and the build explicitly enables Emscripten'\\''s wasmBinary input; a browser check rejects a second fetch. Optional timing records distinguish kernel execution, WASM copies and worker transit without recording arguments or results. **Critique.** One expensive call still occupies its World'\\''s worker and delays later calls. Moving it off the main thread allows painting and input, but does not make the computation cheaper. A shared worker for every World would also make an unrelated World wait; each additional World therefore has its own worker. **Meta-critique.** An async function alone looked like an asynchronous kernel, but synchronous WASM calls and the promise microtask queue kept the entire document demo in one browser task. The boundary measurement and animation-frame check distinguish responsiveness from throughput; both need separate evidence.
EOF
